  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${e}^{x-1}={3}^{x}?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-1} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}\u20613\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}\u20613 \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}\u20613 & =1 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}\u20613) & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{ln}\u20613}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
